Next, the function reads the TIFF image data and extracts metadata about the number of samples per pixel and any extra samples (if present). The function then initializes an OpenSSL cipher object using the `AES_set_encrypt_key` function, which sets the encryption key for the cipher. If this fails, the function returns an error message.

Finally, the function frees the memory allocated for the image data and closes the TIFF file. It then returns a struct containing the parsed TIFF metadata and a pointer to the OpenSSL cipher object. The return type of the function is `int`, which indicates whether the function was successful (0) or not (negative value).

The function uses standard library functions for file handling and error management, ensuring robustness against invalid input and edge cases. It also utilizes the OpenSSL library to perform encryption operations based on a specified command line option. The implementation demonstrates proficiency in both file I/O and cryptographic operations within the OpenSSL library.']

