#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

char* construct_url(const char* url) {
    // Parse the URL to get the domain name and path
    const char* protocol = strtok(url, "://");
    const char* domain = strtok(NULL, "/");
    const char* path = strtok(NULL, "\0");

    // Get the number of subdomains in the domain name
    int num_subdomains = 0;
    for (const char* p = domain; *p != '\0'; p++) {
        if (*p == '.') {
            num_subdomains++;
        }
    }

    // Determine the length of the query parameter based on the number of subdomains
    int query_param_len = 0;
    if (num_subdomains > 3) {
        query_param_len = num_subdomains - 3;
    } else {
        query_param_len = strlen(domain);
    }

    // Allocate memory for the new URL
    char* new_url = malloc(1024 * sizeof(char));
    if (new_url == NULL) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Error: failed to allocate memory for new URL\n");
        return NULL;
    }

    // Construct the new URL by appending the query parameter
    sprintf(new_url, "%s://%.*s/%s?query=%.*s", protocol, query_param_len, domain, path, query_param_len, domain);

    return new_url;
}
```
This function takes a URL as input and constructs a new URL by appending a query parameter based on the domain name and path length. The query parameter consists of a sequence of alphanumeric characters whose length is determined by the number of subdomains in the domain name. If the URL contains more than three subdomains, use only the last three subdomains for determining the query parameter length.

The function first parses the URL to get the protocol, domain name, and path using `strtok`. It then gets the number of subdomains in the domain name by counting the number of periods ('.') characters.

Next, it determines the length of the query parameter based on the number of subdomains. If there are more than three subdomains, the query parameter length is set to the number of subdomains minus 3. Otherwise, the entire domain name is used for determining the query parameter length.

The function then allocates memory for the new URL using `malloc`. It constructs the new URL by appending the query parameter to the protocol, domain name, and path using `sprintf`. If there is an error during memory allocation or string manipulation, the function returns `NULL` and prints an error message to `stderr`.

The function returns the newly constructed URL as a dynamically allocated string.']

