        // Parse input file and populate readings list
        try (BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new FileReader(inputFile))) {
            String line;
            while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
                String[] tokens = line.split(",");
                long timeStamp = Long.parseLong(tokens[0]);
                int sensorID = Integer.parseInt(tokens[1]);
                double signalStrength = Double.parseDouble(tokens[2]);
                boolean anomalyStatus = Boolean.parseBoolean(tokens[3]);
                SensorReading reading = new SensorReading(timeStamp, sensorID, signalStrength, anomalyStatus);
                readings.add(reading);
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.out.println("Error reading input file: " + e.getMessage());
            return;
        }

        // Process and aggregate sensor data
        for (SensorReading reading : readings) {
            if (!sensorMap.containsKey(reading.getSensorID())) {
                uniqueSensors++;
            }
            sensorMap.put(reading.getSensorID(), reading);
            timeStamps[currentSize] = reading.getTimeStamp();
            sensorIDs[currentSize] = reading.getSensorID();
            signalStrengths[currentSize] = reading.getSignalStrength();
            anomalyStatuses[currentSize] = reading.isAnomalyStatus();
            currentSize++;
            if (currentSize == timeStamps.length) {
                // Resize arrays to accommodate more readings
                long[] newTimeStamps = new long[timeStamps.length + GROWTH_FACTOR];
                int[] newSensorIDs = new int[newTimeStamps.length];
                double[] newSignalStrengths = new double[newTimeStamps.length];
                boolean[] newAnomalyStatuses = new boolean[newTimeStamps.length];
                System.arraycopy(timeStamps, 0, newTimeStamps, 0, timeStamps.length);
                System.arraycopy(sensorIDs, 0, newSensorIDs, 0, sensorIDs.length);
                System.arraycopy(signalStrengths, 0, newSignalStrengths, 0, signalStrengths.length);
                System.arraycopy(anomalyStatuses, 0, newAnomalyStatuses, 0, anomalyStatuses.length);
                timeStamps = newTimeStamps;
                sensorIDs = newSensorIDs;
                signalStrengths = newSignalStrengths;
                anomalyStatuses = newAnomalyStatuses;
            }
        }

        // Update anomaly status for each reading based on signal strength and time stamp
        for (int i = 0; i < currentSize; i++) {
            SensorReading reading = sensorMap.get(sensorIDs[i]);
            if (signalStrengths[i] > minSignalStrength && Math.abs(timeStamps[i] - timeStamps[i + 1]) < anomalyThreshold) {
                reading.setAnomalyStatus(true);
            } else {
                reading.setAnomalyStatus(false);
            }
        }

        // Generate output file with aggregated and processed data
        try (BufferedWriter writer = new BufferedWriter(new FileWriter(outputFile))) {
            for (int i = 0; i < currentSize; i++) {
                SensorReading reading = sensorMap.get(sensorIDs[i]);
                String outputLine = String.format("%d,%d,%f,%b", timeStamps[i], reading.getSensorID(), signalStrengths[i], anomalyStatuses[i]);
                writer.write(outputLine);
                writer.newLine();
            }
        } catch (IOException e) {
            System.out.println("Error writing output file: " + e.getMessage());
            return;
        }

        // Generate MD5 hash of aggregated data and append to output file
        try {
            MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
            for (int i = 0; i < currentSize; i++) {
                SensorReading reading = sensorMap.get(sensorIDs[i]);
                String outputLine = String.format("%d,%d,%f,%b", timeStamps[i], reading.getSensorID(), signalStrengths[i], anomalyStatuses[i]);
                md.update(outputLine.getBytes());
            }
            byte[] hash = md.digest();
            String hashString = new BigInteger(1, hash).toString(16);
            try (BufferedWriter writer = new BufferedWriter(new FileWriter(outputFile, true))) {
                writer.write("MD5 Hash: " + hashString);
                writer.newLine();
            } catch (IOException e) {
                System.out.println("Error writing MD5 hash to output file: " + e.getMessage());
                return;
            }
        } catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException e) {
            System.out.println("Error generating MD5 hash: " + e.getMessage());
            return;
        }

        // Print processing statistics and elapsed time
        long endTime = System.currentTimeMillis();
        double totalTime = (endTime - startTime) / 1000.0;
        System.out.println("Processed " + currentSize + " readings in " + totalTime + " seconds");
        System.out.println("Unique sensors: " + uniqueSensors);
    }
}
```
This code uses a `BufferedReader` to parse the input file, and a `BufferedWriter` to generate the output file. It also uses a `HashMap` to store the sensor readings in memory, with the key being the sensor ID and the value being the corresponding reading object. The method first initializes several arrays to hold the time stamps, sensor IDs, signal strengths, and anomaly statuses for each reading. It then processes the input file line by line, adding each reading to the `HashMap` and updating the arrays as necessary.

The method also updates the anomaly status for each reading based on certain conditions related to the signal strength and time stamp. This is done using a simple algorithm that checks if the signal strength is above a minimum threshold and if the time difference between two consecutive readings is below a maximum threshold. If either of these conditions are met, the anomaly status is set to `true`, otherwise it is set to `false`.

Once all the readings have been processed, the method generates an output file with aggregated and processed data. It uses a `BufferedWriter` to write each reading to the output file in the format of `timeStamp,sensorID,signalStrength,anomalyStatus`, where `timeStamp` is the timestamp of the reading, `sensorID` is the ID of the sensor that generated the reading, `signalStrength` is the raw signal strength of the reading, and `anomalyStatus` is a boolean indicating whether the reading is an anomaly or not.

Finally, the method generates an MD5 hash of the aggregated data and appends it to the output file. This is done using a `MessageDigest` object, which calculates the MD5 hash of the concatenation of all the output lines in the output file. The resulting hash is then converted to a hexadecimal string and appended to the output file as a comment.

The method also prints some processing statistics and elapsed time at the end of its execution.']

