The `conditional_log` function supports logging at different verbosity levels (e.g., debug, info, warning, error) and provides an option to temporarily disable logging for specific categories. It also allows configuration of the maximum size of the log file and automatically rotates logs when the specified limit is reached, ensuring that the rotation process is atomic and does not truncate existing logs.

The function also includes a feature to timestamp each log entry for better traceability, allowing for adjustable time zone offsets and handling daylight saving time changes. The log file path can be configured through an environment variable or command-line argument, falling back to a default path if not set, while supporting fallback mechanisms for missing or invalid paths.

Finally, the function integrates with an external logging service that supports remote logging via HTTP POST requests for distributed systems, handling network failures gracefully, including retries with exponential backoff and circuit breaker patterns to prevent resource exhaustion. The function also supports logging to both file and remote destinations simultaneously, managing the flow control between them to ensure balanced throughput and resource usage.

The code adheres to these requirements while maintaining robustness and efficiency under high-load conditions, considering edge cases such as concurrent loggers, varying network conditions, and system failures.']

