This code defines a `struct Record` to store the data for each record in the collection, and a `struct RecordCollection` to store an array of records. The `initRecordCollection()` function reads the JSON data from a file and populates the `records` array with the parsed data. The `printAllRecords()` function iterates over all records in the collection and prints their timestamp, status, and weight. The `filterRecords()` function takes a status as an argument and filters the records based on that status, printing only those records that match the specified status.

The memory usage of this code is optimized by using a fixed-size array for the `records` field in the `struct RecordCollection`, which allows us to avoid dynamic memory allocation and reallocation. The `initRecordCollection()` function also uses a temporary JSON object to parse the data from the file, which helps to reduce memory usage compared to directly parsing the JSON data into the `struct Record` objects.

The time complexity of this code is O(n), where n is the number of records in the file, because we only iterate over the records once when populating the collection and printing them all. The space complexity is also O(n) because we allocate memory for the entire collection at once.']

