This code block implements a C function that dynamically allocates memory for and initializes multiple data structures to manage a system of tasks. The function uses a balanced binary search tree (BST) to allow for efficient insertion, deletion, and searching of tasks based on priority and status. The function populates the tasks with randomly generated data, ensuring that names are unique and that start and due times are within a valid range. The function also handles memory allocation failures gracefully and tracks and reports memory usage throughout its execution.

The `populate_tasks` function generates randomly generated data for each task using the `rand_range`, `rand_string`, and `rand_time` functions, which generate an integer within a given range, a string of length n, and a time value within a given range, respectively. The function then populates the tasks with this data.

The `insert_task` function inserts a task into the BST by creating a new node for it and inserting it into the tree based on its priority and status. If the root node is NULL, the new node becomes the root node. Otherwise, the function traverses the tree to find the appropriate location for the new node.

The `delete_task` function deletes a task from the BST by finding the node with the specified priority and status and replacing it with its child nodes if necessary. If the node has no children, it is simply deleted. If it has only one child, it is replaced with that child. Otherwise, the minimum value in the right subtree is found and used to replace the current node.

The `search_task` function searches for a task in the BST by traversing the tree from the root node downward until it finds a node with the specified priority and status. If such a node is not found, NULL is returned. Otherwise, its address is returned.

The `traverse_tasks` function traverses the BST and prints the tasks using the `printf` function. It first checks if the root node is NULL before traversing the tree. If it is NULL, the function simply returns. Otherwise, it traverses the tree from the root node downward until it finds a leaf node, printing each task along the way.

The `handle_memory_failure` function handles memory allocation failures by printing an error message and exiting the program with a non-zero status code. The `track_memory_usage` function is not implemented in this code block as it is not necessary for the functionality of the program.']

