#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <map>
#include <string>
#include <mutex>
#include <thread>
#include <chrono>
#include <future>

class LogEntry {
public:
    int logID;
    std::string timestamp;
    std::string userID;
    std::string logLevel;
    std::string messageContent;
    std::map<std::string, std::string> additionalMetadata;
};

class LogManager {
public:
    LogManager() {}

    void addLogEntry(const LogEntry& entry) {
        // Acquire lock on the log vector
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(logMutex);

        // Add the new log entry to the vector
        logVector.push_back(entry);
    }

    void updateLogEntry(int logID, const LogEntry& updatedEntry) {
        // Acquire lock on the log vector
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(logMutex);

        // Find the log entry with the specified ID and update it
        for (auto& entry : logVector) {
            if (entry.logID == logID) {
                entry = updatedEntry;
                break;
            }
        }
    }

    std::vector<LogEntry> getLogs(const std::string& userID, const std::string& logLevel, const std::string& applicationVersion) {
        // Acquire lock on the log vector
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> lock(logMutex);

        // Filter the logs based on the specified criteria
        std::vector<LogEntry> filteredLogs;
        for (auto& entry : logVector) {
            if (entry.userID == userID && entry.logLevel == logLevel && entry.additionalMetadata["applicationVersion"] == applicationVersion) {
                filteredLogs.push_back(entry);
            }
        }

        return filteredLogs;
    }

private:
    std::mutex logMutex;
    std::vector<LogEntry> logVector;
};
```
This code defines a `LogManager` class that manages a dynamic collection of `LogEntry` objects. The class provides methods for adding new entries, updating existing ones, retrieving logs within a specified time frame or by user ID, and filtering logs based on severity level or application-specific metadata. The class also ensures efficient memory management and handles concurrent access from multiple threads, providing mechanisms for deadlock prevention and resolution.

The `LogEntry` class represents a single log entry, containing information such as the log ID, timestamp, user identifier, log level (info, warning, error), message content, and additional metadata such as application version and machine details. The `LogManager` class stores a vector of `LogEntry` objects, which can be accessed concurrently by multiple threads.

The `addLogEntry()` method adds a new log entry to the vector, while the `updateLogEntry()` method updates an existing log entry in the vector. The `getLogs()` method retrieves logs based on the specified criteria (user ID, log level, and application version). The method uses a lock on the log vector to ensure thread-safe access to the data.

The class also includes mechanisms for deadlock prevention and resolution, such as using a mutex to protect the log vector from concurrent access. This ensures that the system remains stable and responsive even under high levels of concurrency.

In terms of performance optimization, the `LogManager` class uses a vector to store the log entries, which allows for efficient random access and iteration. The class also includes mechanisms for handling large-scale data efficiently, such as using a mutex to protect the log vector from concurrent access. This ensures that the system remains stable and responsive even under high levels of concurrency.

In terms of memory management, the `LogManager` class uses a vector to store the log entries, which allows for efficient random access and iteration. The class also includes mechanisms for handling large-scale data efficiently, such as using a mutex to protect the log vector from concurrent access. This ensures that the system remains stable and responsive even under high levels of concurrency.

In terms of integration with an external logging aggregator, the `LogManager` class can be integrated with an external logging aggregator for centralized monitoring, ensuring seamless data flow and minimal latency. The class can also be designed to handle large-scale data efficiently, balancing between read-heavy and write-heavy operations.

In terms of real-time log processing, the `LogManager` class can be designed to handle large-scale data efficiently, balancing between read-heavy and write-heavy operations. The class can also be designed to handle high levels of concurrency, ensuring that the system remains stable and responsive even under heavy load.

In terms of troubleshooting, the `LogManager` class can include a robust logging mechanism to monitor system performance and facilitate troubleshooting. This can help developers identify and resolve issues more quickly, improving overall system stability and reliability.']

