The `NetworkMonitor` class is responsible for handling the network monitoring logic. It has a list of network interfaces and a map of packet capture filters, which are used to configure the network interface and filter the packets that are captured. The class also has a timer that is used to start and stop the packet capture process.

The `initNetworkInterfaces` function is used to initialize the network interfaces and start the packet capture process. It first retrieves the list of available network interfaces using `QNetworkInterface::allInterfaces()`. Then, it creates a new instance of `QTcpSocket` for each interface and starts the packet capture process by calling `QTcpSocket::bind()` and `QTcpSocket::connectToHost()`.

The `startPacketCaptureTimer` function is used to start the packet capture timer. It sets the interval of the timer using `QTimer::setInterval()` and starts the timer using `QTimer::start()`. The timer will emit a `packetCaptured()` signal every time a new packet is captured.

The `stopPacketCaptureTimer` function is used to stop the packet capture timer. It stops the timer using `QTimer::stop()`.

The `updateStatistics` function is used to update the network statistics. It takes a `NetworkStatistics` object as an argument and updates the internal statistics accordingly.

The `getStatistics` function is used to get the current network statistics. It returns a reference to the internal `NetworkStatistics` object.

In the GUI, you can use the `NetworkMonitor` class to handle the network monitoring logic. You can create an instance of the class and call its functions to initialize the network interfaces and start the packet capture process. You can also use the `getStatistics()` function to get the current network statistics and display them in a graph or table within the GUI.
```cpp
